Given, velocity of particle is $\mathbf{v}=x \hat{\mathbf{i}}+y t \hat{\mathbf{j}}$

So, $|\mathbf{v}|=v=\sqrt{x^2+y^2 t^2}$
Magnitude of tangential acceleration is

a_t=\frac{d v}{d t}=\frac{0+2 t y^2}{2 \sqrt{x^2+y^2 t^2}}

or $\quad a_t=\frac{t y^2}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2 t^2}}$
Now, substituting $t=\frac{x \sqrt{3}}{y}$, we get

a_t=\frac{\frac{x \sqrt{3}}{y} \times y^2}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2 \times\left(\frac{x^2 \times 3}{y^2}\right)}}

\Rightarrow \quad a_t=\frac{\sqrt{3} y}{2}

Also, total acceleration of particle is $\mathbf{a}=\frac{d v}{d t}$
\begin{array}{ll}
\Rightarrow & \mathbf{a}=\frac{d}{d t}(x \hat{\mathbf{i}}+y t \hat{\mathbf{j}}) \\
\Rightarrow & \mathbf{a}=y \hat{\mathbf{j}}
\end{array}

or magnitude of total acceleration is

a=|\mathbf{a}|=y

Hence, normal acceleration is

\mathbf{a}_n=\mathbf{a}_{\text {total }}-\mathbf{a}_{\text {tangential }}

So, $a_n=\left|\mathbf{a}_n\right|=$ magnitude of normal acceleration

\begin{aligned}
& =\sqrt{a^2-a_t^2}=\sqrt{y^2-\left(\frac{y^4 t^2}{x^2+y^2 t^2}\right)} \\
& =\sqrt{\frac{x^2 y^2}{x^2+y^2 t^2}}=\frac{x y}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2 t^2}}
\end{aligned}

Now with $t=\frac{x \sqrt{3}}{y}$, we get

a_n=\left|\mathbf{a}_n\right|=\frac{x y}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2 \cdot \frac{3 x^2}{y^2}}}=\frac{x y}{2 x}=\frac{y}{2}

