So, $\quad m=\frac{\pi r^2 l \cdot d}{l}$, where, $d=$ density and

m=\pi r^2 d \text {. }

Given, $f_A$ (first overtone, $n=2$ )

\begin{aligned}
& =f_B(\text { second overtone, } n=3) \\
\Rightarrow \frac{2}{2 l_A} \sqrt{\frac{T}{m_A}} & =\frac{3}{2 l_B} \sqrt{\frac{T}{m_B}} \Rightarrow \frac{1}{l_A \sqrt{\pi r_A^2 d}}=\frac{3}{2 l_B \sqrt{\pi r_B^2 d}} \\
